  <page>
    <title>Modular origami</title>
    <id>246092</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40391788</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-20T05:58:22Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>165.82.76.201</ip>
      </contributor>
      <comment>/* External links */</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{cleanup-date|December 2005}}

''Modular Origami'' is a form of [[origami]] that uses multiple small interlocking modules to create a larger model, usually a [[polyhedron]] or other geometric form. Its origins lie with the traditional Japanese [[kusudama]], or medicine ball, in which the modules are strung together with thread to form a sphere that was thought to have several various benevolent properties. Each module requires a separate piece of paper; consequently, models can use anything from three to 10,375 individual sheets (the latter was a modular cat folded by [[Lluis Bigas]])

Most models are fixed together either by inserting preexisting flaps on the module into pockets in the module, or by the use of smaller pieces of paper which are folded and then inserted into pockets to join the modules together, a technique used extensively by [[Tomoko Fuse]] in her book &quot;Unit Origami&quot;

The most common type of Modular Origami depicts geometric forms, such as [[polyhedra]]. For Geometric Modules, each module corresponds to part of a polyhedron; there are modules based on edges, sides, and vertices, although most models will only have one type. These modules can be used to make any polyhedron.

The term ‘Ball Origami’ can also used to describe a few types of modular origami, mostly [[geodesic dome|geodesics]] and other large, irregular shapes. This term, contrary to the belief of many, is not a synonym of Modular origami, as modular origami can also be used to create tessellations of geometric forms and more complex three-dimensional models of other things that do not resemble spheres.

Sometimes other origami models are made with many sheets of paper that have been folded differently, such as many of joseph wu's larger works, but this is usually frowned upon and does not belong to the category of modular origami.

==External links==

* [http://origami.kvi.nl/models/ornament/starkusd/ Star Kusudama Instructions]
* [http://www.ask.ne.jp/~kanzasi/en/e-kusu.html Kusudama Pictures]
* [http://home.comcast.net/~meenaks/diagrams/ Folding Instructions For Many Polyhedra and Variations]
* [http://www.jillsart.com/SectionIndex.aspx?sectionID=modular.origami Some Artistic Examples]

[[Category:Origami]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>NonStop SQL</title>
    <id>246093</id>
    <revision>
      <id>16122961</id>
      <timestamp>2005-03-05T08:00:42Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Minghong</username>
        <id>110454</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>/* History */  Add see also</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''NonStop SQL''' is a [[relational database]] product originally produced at [[Tandem Computers]] using the pioneering [[Ingres]] source code from [[University of California, Berkeley]]. NonStop is the &quot;brand name&quot; for Tandem's suite of high-availabiliy products, a brand that survived Tandem's takeover by [[Compaq]], and today after Compaq's takeover by [[Hewlett-Packard|HP]]. NonStop SQL continues to be offered as a part of this suite today.

==History==
Jerry Held and Karel Youssefi were both team members on the original Ingres project under [[Michael Stonebraker]], who moved to Tandem in the early 1980s to commercialize the Ingres code. NonStop SQL was a version of Ingres modified to run effectively on parallel computers, adding functionality for distributed data, distributed execution, and distributed transactions (the last being fairly difficult).

First released in 1987, a second version in 1989 added the ability to run queries in parallel, and the product became fairly famous for being one of the few systems that scales almost linearly with the number of processors in the machine: adding a second CPU to an existing NonStop SQL server will almost exactly double its performance. Tandem was later purchased by Compaq who started a re-write in 2000, and now the product is at HP.

==See also==
*[[List of relational database management systems]]
*[[Comparison of relational database management systems]]

[[Category:Database management systems]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Healesville Sanctuary</title>
    <id>246100</id>
    <revision>
      <id>29686486</id>
      <timestamp>2005-11-30T11:49:43Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>203.45.13.175</ip>
      </contributor>
      <comment>revert vandalism by 212.219.240.201</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Healesville Sanctuary''' is a [[zoo]] specializing in native [[Australia]]n animals. The zoo is located at [[Healesville, Victoria|Healesville]] on the outskirts of [[Melbourne, Australia|Melbourne]], [[Victoria (Australia)|Victoria]], [[Australia]], and has a history of breeding native animals; it is one of two places to have successfully bred a [[platypus]], the other being [[Sydney]]'s [[Taronga Zoo]].

The zoo is set in [[bushland]] where paths wind through different habitat areas showcasing [[wallabies]], [[kangaroo]]s, and over 200 [[List of Australian birds|native bird]] varieties.

Conducted tours, bird shows and interpretive skill are available to visitors.

Dr. Colin MacKenzie (knighted in 1929) set up the Institute of Anatomical Research in 1920 on 78 acres (316,000&amp;nbsp;m&amp;sup2;) of land which had formerly been part of the Coranderrk Aboriginal Reserve.  The Reserve passed to the Healesville Council in 1927 and became the Sir Colin MacKenzie Sanctuary in 1934.

The first platypus bred in captivity was born at the Sanctuary in 1943.

==External links==

*[http://www.zoo.org.au/visiting.cfm?zoo_id=2 Healesville Sanctuary]

[[Category:Zoos in Australia]]</text>
    </revision>
