  <page>
    <title>Transitivity of identity</title>
    <id>246183</id>
    <revision>
      <id>16123045</id>
      <timestamp>2004-09-01T01:50:03Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Paul August</username>
        <id>87355</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>fixed link</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">The '''transitivity of identity''' is the [[logic]]al principle that, if a=b, and b=c, then a=c.

For example, if you know that the [[Morning Star]] is the same thing as the Evening Star, and you know that the Evening Star is [[Venus (planet)|Venus]], you can conclude that the Morning Star is Venus.

The transitivity of identity (like rules concerning identity generally) is not always valid in [[epistemology|epistemological]], [[modality|modal]], or otherwise [[opaque]] [[intensional context|context]]s. For example, if [[Oedipus]] knows that his wife is [[Jocasta]], and you know that Jocasta is the mother of Oedipus, you may '''not''' conclude that Oedipus knows that his wife is his mother.

''See also:'' [[transitive relation]], [[identity]]

[[Category:logic]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Superhuman</title>
    <id>246184</id>
    <revision>
      <id>39428340</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-13T01:56:14Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>JeremyA</username>
        <id>170381</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Reverted edits by [[Special:Contributions/205.188.116.68|205.188.116.68]] ([[User talk:205.188.116.68|talk]]) to last version by Damicatz</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{cleanup-date|June 2005}}

A '''superhuman''' is an entity with intelligence or abilities exceeding normal human standards. 

''Superhuman'' can mean an &quot;improved&quot; human, for example, by [[gene]]tic modification, or as what humans might evolve into, in the distant future. Occasionally, it could mean a &quot;normal&quot; human with unusual abilities, such as [[parapsychology|psychic]] abilities or exceptional proficiency at something.

''Superhuman'' can also mean something that isn't human, but considered to be &quot;superior&quot; to humans in some ways. A robot that easily passed the [[Turing test]], and could do some things humans can't, could be considered superhuman. A very [[intelligence (trait)|intelligent]] or [[physical strength|strong]] [[Extraterrestrial life|alien]] could be considered superhuman.

The concept of the superhuman is quite popular in [[science fiction]], where superhumans are often [[mutant]]s or [[genetic engineering|genetically engineered]]. The greatest publicity of the concept is, of course, comic book [[superhero]]es, such as [[Superman]], though this is not as well-thought-out as is [[Superhumans in science fiction|science fiction genre of superhuman]]s. The term is often used in discussions of comic book characters because the term [[Superman]] is registered as a [[trademark]]. Superhuman characters in various [[comics]], [[role-playing games]] and other entertainment mediums have also been referred to as [[metahuman]] or [[Posthuman (Human evolution)|posthuman]].

In [[Marvel Comics]] the term is part of a power classification system and applies to aptitude (usually physical) far beyond the range attainable by normal human beings. An '''athlete''' is in extraordinary physical condition, such as a weight lifter or boxer. '''Peak human''' is applied to physical abilities that are nearly beyond the limits of humans. '''Enhanced human''' refers to abilities some distance beyond the limits of humans, such as beng able to lift a small car but not a tank, and is a kind of term for light superhuman abilities. Then is the term [[superhuman]]. Characters with a superhuman attribute are far beyond normal human abilities. There is also a range beyond superhuman and this is [[metahuman]].  As the enhanced human level is really just a term for a low superhuman ability, metahuman is a term for a high superhuman ability.

==Superhumans in Science Fiction==
One of the most significant types of science fiction story derives from the concept of [[mutation]]. It's that a human being would mutate into a being that either has powers as yet not exhibited by humans, or else motivations entirely different from those human beings, or both. 

One of the problems with the concept is that it is dominated by a misunderstanding of the theory of [[evolution]]. Mutation takes place, but it is only a summation of the interactions with the environment which leads first to a new [[species]], and then some time has to occur before one species becomes extinct while another, not necessarily the mutated species, survives. And it is only a value-judgement by humans which declares that the different characteristic of new species is &quot;superior&quot;. But the notion of [[progress]] is inherently built into this genre of stories. 

Examples
*''[[Odd John]]'' by [[Olaf Stapledon]] is an early example of the genre.
* ''[[Slan]]'', by [[A. E. van Vogt]], features two types of beings, one with psychic powers.
* [[Ludens]] featured in [[Boris and Arkady Strugatsky]]'s [[Noon Universe]] is a typical example for a superhuman race

==Human extinction==
One reason proposed for a possible [[human extinction]] is that [[human]]s will evolve into superhumans, leaving no trace of the original humans.

==See also==
* [[Posthuman (Human evolution)|Posthuman]]
* [[Subhuman]]
* [[Superhero]]
* [[Superman]]
* [[Supervillain]]
* [[Transhuman]]
* [[Übermensch]]

[[Category:Science fiction themes]]
[[simple:Superhuman]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
